# =====================================================
# internal/ — server-to-server only (Next.js kernel <-> PHP session bridge).
#
# Default posture: deny ALL requests via the public Apache vhost. This is a
# conservative fail-closed default, not the real security boundary — the
# actual mechanism that lets the Next.js kernel (packages/auth's
# bridgeClient.ts) reach internal/session-bridge.php is Docker-internal-
# network isolation / an nginx `internal;` location, which is mig-infra's
# job (plan Phase 0/13 strangler edge work), not this file's. Until that
# lands, session-bridge.php is also HMAC-signature-gated on every request
# (see its own top-of-file comment) as defense-in-depth underneath this.
#
# mig-infra: once the internal Docker network CIDR is known, replace the
# blanket deny below with an `Allow from <that CIDR>` (Apache 2.2) /
# `Require ip <that CIDR>` (Apache 2.4) scoped rule — do not simply delete
# this file.
#
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# mig-infra, 2026-07-12 — infra/e2e/ harness carve-out (Phase 1 batch 3).
#
# The rule below scopes access to 172.30.99.0/24 ONLY — the FIXED subnet
# infra/e2e/docker-compose.yml's dedicated `e2e-net` bridge network declares
# (see that file's `networks.e2e-net.ipam` block). It exists so the E2E
# bridge harness's Node runner (infra/e2e/run.mjs, running on the HOST, not
# inside any container) can reach this container's published port and POST
# to internal/session-bridge.php — verified empirically: a host-originated
# request to a container's published port on a custom bridge network is
# SNAT'd to that network's gateway IP (172.30.99.1 for this subnet), which
# is why the whole /24 is allow-listed, not a single host IP.
#
# This is a NARROW, HARNESS-SCOPED carve-out for a throwaway local/CI
# compose project — it is NOT the production Phase 0 cutover decision. The
# real production CIDR (whatever Docker network Phase 0's docker-compose
# actually runs the `php` + Next.js kernel containers on) is a SEPARATE
# decision tracked in docs/plans/2026-07-12-nextjs-full-migration-plan.md
# Phase 0/13 — do not assume 172.30.99.0/24 means anything there, and do not
# widen this rule to cover it. When that production CIDR is decided, add a
# SECOND scoped rule alongside this one (or replace it, if the harness is
# retired) rather than loosening this one.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Apache 2.4
<IfModule mod_authz_core.c>
    Require ip 172.30.99.0/24
</IfModule>

# Apache 2.2 (mod_access_compat fallback)
<IfModule !mod_authz_core.c>
    Order deny,allow
    Deny from all
    Allow from 172.30.99.0/24
</IfModule>
